# Introduction to Computer Networks

> A network is computers connected so they can exchange data. This
> lesson covers how data finds its way from one machine to another —
> the plumbing behind every web page and message.

## 1. Network Types by Scale

| Type | Scope | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| PAN | A few metres | Bluetooth headset to phone |
| LAN | Building / campus | Home Wi-Fi, office ethernet |
| MAN | City | Cable network |
| WAN | Country / world | The Internet itself |

## 2. The Client–Server Idea

Most traffic follows a simple pattern:

- A **client** (your browser) *requests* a resource.
- A **server** (a distant computer) *responds* with it.

Your browser asking for a page sends an HTTP request; the server replies
with HTML. Peer-to-peer networks blur the roles — every machine is both
client and server.

## 3. IP Addresses and DNS

[definition]
An **IP address** is a numeric label identifying a device on a network
(IPv4 looks like `192.168.1.10`; IPv6 like `2001:db8::8a2e:370:7334`).
**DNS (Domain Name System)** translates human-friendly names such as
`example.com` into those addresses.
[/definition]

When you visit a site, DNS resolution happens first:

@diagram tree id="dns-steps" layout="tree" aria="Steps from URL to server"
  "Type example.com" -> "Ask DNS resolver"
  "Ask DNS resolver" -> "Get 93.184.216.34"
  "Get 93.184.216.34" -> "Send HTTP request"
  "Send HTTP request" -> "Server responds with page"
@/diagram

## 4. Packets and Switching

Data does not travel whole. It is chopped into **packets** — small
chunks carrying source/destination addresses and sequence numbers.
Packets may take different routes and are reassembled at the destination.
This **packet switching** is what makes the Internet robust: if one path
fails, others carry the traffic.

## 5. Layers: Who Does What

| Layer | Job | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Application | Meaningful services to programs | HTTP, DNS, SMTP |
| Transport | Delivery between programs; reliability | TCP, UDP |
| Internet | Addressing and routing between hosts | IP |
| Link | Delivery over one physical hop | Ethernet, Wi-Fi |

- **TCP** guarantees ordered, complete delivery (web pages, email).
- **UDP** just sends — fast, no promises (video calls, games).

[info title="Latency vs bandwidth"]
**Bandwidth** is how much data fits through per second (a wide pipe);
**latency** is how long one packet takes to arrive (pipe length).
Downloads care about bandwidth; video calls care about latency.
[/info]

## Key Takeaways

- Networks scale from PAN to WAN; the Internet is a WAN of WANs.
- DNS turns names into IP addresses before any request is sent.
- Data moves as packets that can be rerouted around damage.
- TCP trades speed for reliability; UDP trades reliability for speed.
